WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.199
Maddy: In this video we are going to look
at monitoring discipline and how can we

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.770
identify the interpersonal behaviour or
support the skills required by a manger.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.700
Phillip: Your organisation should have
policies in place to guide you with resolving

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:18.640
disciplinary issues, so it is important that
you take time to familiarise yourself with them.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:23.689
Maddy: Disciplinary issues at work are
usually to do with an employee’s conduct

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:27.900
or with their capability, or ability
to do their work to the right standard.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:31.910
So what behaviours and skills do you need?

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:37.079
Phillip: There are interpersonal skills which can
be developed to help you monitor the discipline

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:42.990
in your team and in general it’s best to try and
resolve disciplinary issues informally at first,

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:49.600
if appropriate Having these skills can often help
avoid having to use formal mechanisms wherever

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.010
possible, but only when not deemed necessary.

00:00:54.329 --> 00:00:56.909
Maddy: Coaching and mentoring are
great skills and behaviours to have

00:00:56.949 --> 00:00:58.749
when it comes to capability issues.

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:04.019
Phillip: Coaching involves guiding someone
to help them achieve their goals and

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:09.919
potential and mentoring involves sharing
your knowledge, skills, and experience with

00:01:09.919 --> 00:01:12.379
someone so they can develop their own skillset.

00:01:13.459 --> 00:01:18.189
These are useful for dealing with issues
of capability, when the first action should

00:01:18.189 --> 00:01:22.509
be to help them in achieving the expected
standard and not punish them for failing.

00:01:23.269 --> 00:01:27.719
Maddy: Coaching and mentoring are powerful
tools in enabling personal professional

00:01:27.719 --> 00:01:29.849
development and career development.

00:01:30.679 --> 00:01:36.139
Personal professional development involves
assessing goals and aims, and the skills and

00:01:36.139 --> 00:01:41.909
abilities needed to achieve them, and this covers
general life goals as well as career goals.

00:01:43.139 --> 00:01:47.809
Phillip: Career development is similar,
though specifically focused on career goals.

00:01:48.709 --> 00:01:53.189
Having the ability to coach and mentor
your team will not only help them through

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:58.809
capability issues but will also empower their
personal professional and career development.

00:01:59.949 --> 00:02:02.699
Maddy: So how do we approach
difficult conversations?

00:02:03.249 --> 00:02:07.799
When talking to people about conduct
and capability it can be tricky as

00:02:07.799 --> 00:02:09.529
it touches on sensitive subjects.

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:15.429
Being able to have difficult conversations is an
important skill for team leaders and managers,

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:17.799
and there’s different methods for doing this.

00:02:18.609 --> 00:02:22.779
Phillip: You should be calm and empathetic
during the conversation, making sure you

00:02:22.789 --> 00:02:27.689
listen when the other person expresses their
feelings and point of view about the situation.

00:02:28.419 --> 00:02:34.379
Be prepared by writing a list of key points
you want to cover, to stay on topic, also stick

00:02:34.379 --> 00:02:39.919
to the facts as much as possible, rather than
being drawn into “She said/ He said” debates.

00:02:40.689 --> 00:02:44.499
Keep the atmosphere positive and
focussed on fixing the situation,

00:02:44.729 --> 00:02:46.399
rather than apportioning blame.

00:02:47.189 --> 00:02:51.229
Maddy: Provide clear feedback is often
related to difficult conversations

00:02:51.869 --> 00:02:53.879
but is also a skill, in its own right.

00:02:54.639 --> 00:02:58.849
When giving someone feedback about their
conduct or performance, that feedback should

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.829
be precise and not open to misinterpretation.

00:03:02.559 --> 00:03:07.049
Try to stick to the facts as much as
possible, even using data if its available.

00:03:07.619 --> 00:03:10.789
Also think about what’s the best
way to give someone feedback if

00:03:10.789 --> 00:03:12.129
it’s about something sensitive.

00:03:12.889 --> 00:03:15.599
Better to do it in person rather than by email.

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:18.059
Phillip: Organisational skills

00:03:19.029 --> 00:03:24.389
As a team leader, you’re responsible for making
sure your team stays organised and efficient.

00:03:25.009 --> 00:03:28.529
Workplace conflicts that lead to
disciplinary issues are often caused

00:03:28.529 --> 00:03:30.529
by teams being poorly organised.

00:03:31.569 --> 00:03:36.569
By taking steps to make sure your team
is well organised; everyone has a clearly

00:03:36.569 --> 00:03:41.909
defined job role, there are strong lines of
communication, and people understand the goals

00:03:41.909 --> 00:03:46.959
they’re expected to achieve; your team will
be much less prone to disciplinary issues.

00:03:47.869 --> 00:03:52.129
Maddy: Spend time each day thinking about how
your team are going to achieve their tasks.

00:03:52.499 --> 00:03:54.229
What problems can you anticipate?

00:03:54.579 --> 00:03:56.279
What’s gone right or wrong before?

00:03:56.889 --> 00:04:02.429
Doing small things like making ‘To Do lists’
is a great way of staying better organised

00:04:02.789 --> 00:04:06.869
and the more organised you are, the better
the example you’re setting for your team.

00:04:07.769 --> 00:04:08.229
Phillip: Empathy

00:04:09.739 --> 00:04:12.223
Disciplinary issues often
have an emotional component.

00:04:13.243 --> 00:04:17.313
The standard of someone’s work may have
declined because of emotional trouble or

00:04:17.313 --> 00:04:22.113
their conduct may be inappropriate because of
other frustrations elsewhere in their life.

00:04:22.983 --> 00:04:28.023
Maddy: When responding to disciplinary issues,
try putting yourself in the other person’s shoes.

00:04:28.723 --> 00:04:32.993
Showing understanding of what it feels like
to be the other person and their situation

00:04:33.323 --> 00:04:37.463
can help you gauge the right response and
help to figure out why it’s happening.

00:04:38.613 --> 00:04:42.923
Phillip: Being more empathetic and understanding
the feeling of others can make you a more

00:04:42.923 --> 00:04:45.023
observant and approachable team leader.

00:04:45.673 --> 00:04:50.903
This also enables your team to feel you are
more approachable and can help make disciplinary

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:53.333
issues less likely to occur and escalate.

00:04:54.423 --> 00:04:55.233
Maddy: Safeguarding

00:04:55.553 --> 00:05:01.183
Disciplinary issues can also be due to complicated
causes that are not under the person’s control.

00:05:01.793 --> 00:05:04.233
These may be inside or outside of work.

00:05:04.983 --> 00:05:09.393
By investigating what’s going on, you might
find that there are safeguarding issues.

00:05:10.283 --> 00:05:14.663
Phillip: These can occur when someone is
judged to be vulnerable due to age, disability,

00:05:14.953 --> 00:05:21.423
illness, or an inability to protect themselves
from abuse or communicate abuse appears to

00:05:21.433 --> 00:05:24.403
be or actually is suffering abuse or neglect.

00:05:25.263 --> 00:05:28.983
If there are members of your team who are
considered vulnerable adults, you should be

00:05:28.983 --> 00:05:33.733
especially mindful of their status, if there
are disciplinary issues involving them, as

00:05:33.953 --> 00:05:36.033
this could be a sign of safeguarding issues.

00:05:36.993 --> 00:05:42.473
Maddy: You should immediately escalate this to HR
and to law enforcement or other external bodies,

00:05:42.473 --> 00:05:47.353
if necessary, especially if there is reason
to believe there is physical or sexual abuse.

00:05:47.853 --> 00:05:51.663
You can be prosecuted for failing in
your safeguarding duties, so it is

00:05:51.663 --> 00:05:53.963
important to treat this very seriously.

